RED FLAG 13 — PRODUCT NAME FROM EXCEL, NOT UI FIELD (EI + RS + DEG/EG)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Bug: UI product-name field is unreliable ("test", "abc", "asdfghjkl",
"Esome", "5mg120mg"). Current logic tries to use it, then guards gibberish.
Better: don't depend on the UI field for identity at all.

Fix: Derive the product/subject identity from the Excel composition FIRST row
(the API / first quantitative non-q.s. ingredient), NOT from the UI field.
Priority for the document's product identity:
  1. Excel API row ingredient name (Function contains "active"), else the
     first quantitative non-q.s. row's ingredient name.
  2. UI product-name field ONLY if it is non-empty AND not gibberish
     (len ≥ 4, has a vowel, not in {test,abc,xxx,demo,sample,asdf,qwerty},
      not a pure dose string like "5mg120mg").
  3. Fallback: first Excel row name.
Never pick a gibberish/placeholder UI value over a real Excel ingredient.
The UI field becomes an OPTIONAL override, not the source of truth.
Applies to EI, RS, DEG/EG (Excel-driven). BE NOC excluded (no composition
Excel — keep its metadata product name).

Acceptance:
- Vaso EI, UI="Vasopressin" (valid) → used.
- Carboprost EI, UI="Test" → ignored; product = "Carboprost Tromethamine"
  (Excel row 1).
- Chlorpromazine RS, UI="CHLORPROMAZINE HYDROCHLORIDE" (valid) → used; if UI
  were "test" → falls back to Excel row 1 "Chlorpromazine Hydrochloride".
- Never emit a placeholder as the product/title.

RED FLAG 14 — EXCEL COLUMN SELECTION: RELEVANT FIELDS ONLY (ALL EXCEL TYPES)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Bug: Parser picked "Pharmaceutical Function" as the ingredient-name column
(Chlorpromazine) → "Antioxidant"/"Tonicity agent" as names → empty doc.
General rule needed: only the RELEVANT composition columns are consumed;
decorative / metadata / role columns are dropped elegantly.

Fix (RS + EI + DEG/EG composition parser):
Whitelist-then-exclude column classification by header semantics:
  INGREDIENT-NAME col  ← "ingredient","material name","name of ingredient",
                          "component","raw material"  (highest priority)
  MATERIAL-CODE col    ← "material code","item code","sap code"
  QUANTITY col(s)      ← "qty","quantity","mg/ml","/ml","/vial","/tab","/unit",
                          "amount","per 1ml","per 2ml","strength"
  FUNCTION/ROLE col    ← "function","pharmaceutical function","role","category",
                          "purpose"   → read as ROLE (q.s./active detection),
                          NEVER as ingredient name
  REFERENCE/STD col    ← "reference","quality standard","grade","compendia"
                          → metadata only
  VENDOR col           ← "vendor","supplier","manufacturer"  → supplier field
  DROP silently        ← unlabeled decorative columns, S.No/serial, blank
                          headers, banner/subtitle/notes rows, merged title
                          rows, filter-dropdown rows
Rules:
- The ingredient-name column must NEVER be a header containing function/role/
  category/purpose/reference/vendor.
- If multiple columns qualify as ingredient-name, pick the leftmost pure-name
  column (not a code, not a function).
- Skip banner/subtitle/notes rows (merged cell spanning many cols, or prose
  >60 chars, or starting with "Note"/"*"/"q.s.").
- Self-test FAIL if resolved ingredient names are role words
  (antioxidant/tonicity/buffer/vehicle/diluent/active/lubricant/binder as a
  NAME) — signals wrong-column pick.
Apply to every doc type's Excel and to edge cases (extra columns, reordered
columns, non-standard headers, decorative title banners, Qty/1mL+Qty/2mL,
multi-strength tablet columns).

Acceptance:
- Chlorpromazine (has Function col) → 7 real ingredients, Function read as role.
- Vaso/Carboprost ("Quantity per mL/Vial") → quantities read; function/reference
  columns dropped; no role word becomes a name.

